{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And if you make me an altar of stones, you shall not build them of hewn stones, for you have lifted your sword over it.</b> By this HaShem indicated that He wished to demonstrate matters of humility through this altar, and the sword signifies pride, as it is written (Deuteronomy 33:29), ״וַאֲשֶׁר חֶרֶב גַּאֲוָתֶךָ״. Therefore the lifting of the sword profanes it. And He sealed the boundary of humility with the statement ״וְלֹא תַעֲלֶה בְמַעֲלֹת עַל מִזְבְּחִי״ and so forth. For ascending by steps is from rank to rank, and in this ascent there is a way of pride and grandeur, and it is impossible for this ascent to be a way of arrogance in the place of ״זִבְחֵי אֱלֹהִים רוּחַ נִשְׁבָּרָה״ (Psalms 51:19). And He gave a reason for the matter: ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא תִגָּלֶה עֶרְוָתְךָ עָלָיו״. Although the verse is according to its plain sense, none the less it also contains a hint that every way of pride and grandeur contains an aspect of uncovered nakedness, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Sotah 4b), ״כָּל הַמִּתְגָּאֶה כְּאִלּוּ בָּא עַל כָּל הָעֲרָיוֹת״ and so forth, and they said further there, ״כָּל הַמִּתְגָּאֶה לְסוֹף נִכְשָׁל בְּאֵשֶׁת אִישׁ״, and the reason for this matter will be explained with the help of HaShem in the portion of Korach at the verse ״וַיִּשְׁמַע מֹשֶׁה וַיִּפֹּל עַל פָּנָיו״ (Numbers 16:4). And our Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 110a) that they suspected him concerning another man's wife, and this is because they said that all his deeds were for the sake of self-aggrandizement, as it is written (Numbers 16:3) ״וּמַדּוּעַ תִּתְנַשְּׂאוּ עַל קְהַל ה׳״ — ״וַיִּשְׁמַע מֹשֶׁה״ and so forth: he understood this from their words, when they said ״כִּי כָל הָעֵדָה כֻּלָּם קְדוֹשִׁים, וּמַדּוּעַ תִּתְנַשְּׂאוּ?״, for in every place where there is holiness there is also the boundary of nakedness, and if so — ״מַדּוּעַ תִּתְנַשְּׂאוּ״ to profane their holiness by means of nakedness? Therefore ״וַיִּשְׁמַע מֹשֶׁה״, and there the entire matter and its reason are explained. And in the adjacent portion of Mishpatim (Exodus 21:1) it will be explained that for this reason the seat of the Sanhedrin was beside the altar, so that they would be likened one to the other, and whoever disqualifies in the altar also disqualifies in the judges. And for this reason our Sages of blessed memory expounded (Jerusalem Talmud, Bikkurim 3:3) the verse ״לֹא תַעֲשׂוּן אִתִּי אֱלֹהֵי כֶסֶף״ (Exodus 20:20) concerning judges who are unfit who were appointed for the sake of silver and gold and possess no spirit at all; therefore we have found a place to explain the entire portion regarding the appointment of fit judges."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:22:08.575477396Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}